#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189032} :activity/id #uuid "991198d2-0d13-559b-aa50-a822d61cae0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050282 :topic/id #uuid "3c94ddf9-ba20-52cc-bd59-5aeaadb59184" :topic/math-academy-id 3868} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189493 :tutorial/content "We can manipulate units the same way as variables, which often provides a convenient way to solve problems. One type of problem where units can help is converting between different units of measurement.\n\nFor example, let's use units to convert $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ into grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nSince we're converting from kilograms to grams, we need a conversion between these two units. According to our table, there are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our **conversion factor.** It's important to write the conversion factor with the units included.\n\nTo convert $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ to grams, we multiply this quantity by our conversion factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.03kg &= 0.03kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the kilogram units $(\\textrm{kg})$ cancel, leaving only gram units $(\\textrm{g})$. Consequently, our expression can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.03kg &= 0.03kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.03kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.03 \\cdot 1000g \\\\\n&= 30g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $30 \\,\\text{g}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When multiplying by the conversion factor, the units we want to remove should *always* cancel, and the units we wish to keep should *always* remain. If this isn't the case, we've made a mistake somewhere.\n- We can think of the conversion factor as equivalent to the number $1$, and multiplying a quantity by $1$ doesn't change it. Therefore, multiplying $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by the conversion factor doesn't change the *size* of the quantity, though it does change the units.\n\nFinally, notice that, in this example, we've converted from a \"larger\" unit of mass $(\\textrm{kg})$ to a smaller one $(\\textrm{g})$. We'll see how to use units to convert from smaller units of mass to larger units later in this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a61c3c5b-cabe-50e6-bd8c-c451fc7f7329" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8735 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a5a6a7de-dd8f-533d-8cb9-defc2774fef3" :step/math-academy-id 142504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188179 :question/id #uuid "a8a6ddf1-4401-5f68-9eba-e6d1162d33eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-14051"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12bd9bab-79dd-577e-907e-5f537b3b5eac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188958}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Kilograms to Grams"} :step/id #uuid "3b9c48be-9be5-5ad9-8524-1db31336c531" :step/math-academy-id 142505 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188180 :question/id #uuid "f565f22a-58de-5553-b618-d55f088be079" :question/math-academy-id "e-14052"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "278e33dd-6216-5cf9-9e52-e20924e0622b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188921}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Grams to Milligrams"} :step/id #uuid "d06d61a6-e2a7-5788-ac3a-dc0591173d8d" :step/math-academy-id 142509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189495 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use units to convert from larger units of mass to smaller ones. Let's now learn how to convert from smaller units to larger ones.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $600\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm {mg}$ per $\\textrm{g}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{g}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm{mg})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{g})$, we construct our conversion factor by taking the *reciprocal* of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n600mg &= 600mg \\cdot \\frac{g}{1000mg} \\\\\n&= 600mg \\cdot \\frac{g}{1000mg} \\\\\n&= 600 \\cdot \\frac{g}{1000} \\\\\n&= \\frac{600}{1000}g \\\\\n&= 0.6g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ is equivalent to $0.6\\,\\text{g}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we say that grams are a \"larger unit of mass\" than milligrams, this simply means that $1\\,\\textrm g$ is a larger mass than $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$.\n- If you forget to take the reciprocal to find the conversion factor, the units will not cancel when you multiply. This indicates that a mistake has occurred. In such cases, it's best to double-check that you're using the correct conversion factor." :tutorial/id #uuid "295b5ac0-e435-50fd-9493-4e753d8c31bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8739 :tutorial/title "Using Reciprocals to Convert From Smaller Units to Larger Units"} :step/id #uuid "a44cd1ff-e34d-5106-a89c-28301fc8fca6" :step/math-academy-id 142513 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188182 :question/id #uuid "8e2f9e3e-e0b3-53d5-88e6-38cbcb37af59" :question/math-academy-id "e-14057"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0018c984-790b-57d0-b086-77f5bdf8f273" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188877}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Smaller Units to Larger Units"} :step/id #uuid "2178685b-38fc-5e05-805e-7615b35b6f75" :step/math-academy-id 142514}] :activity/title "Unit Conversions Using Base Units of Mass" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188462}]}
